pnpm typecheck   # clean
pnpm check       # oxfmt + oxlint + type-aware, clean
pnpm vitest run tests/integration/cloudflare-run-reaper.test.ts \
  tests/integration/admitted-agent-lane-runtime.test.ts \
  tests/integration/cloudflare-run-async-driver.test.ts \
  tests/integration/cloudflare-sandbox-agent-lanes.test.ts   # 12 + 4 + 18 + 25 = 59 pass
pnpm test:integration   # full suite, exit 0
```

## Burn-In Gate

Per the Phase 2 D3 acceptance bar, the 10-run count stays at **zero**. All three
layers are now deployed; a fresh dream-shape run must reach `captured`
**observed live on deployed code** before the burn-in restarts. The post-B
re-drive already taught the lesson Layer C encodes: a bounded pi is necessary but
not sufficient — the *tail* and the *reaper* both had to learn to speak. With C,
the next re-drive is the real instrument: the planner phase either **completes to
`captured`**, or it **surfaces a marker** — whether pi self-bounded (124 with its
stderr tail) or the tail itself wedged (the reaper's SIGTERM now names the failing
step and carries pi's stderr regardless). There is no remaining path by which the
blocker is causeless; `adapter_unavailable` with `nodes=0` and an empty stderr is
no longer reachable. Whatever the next run blocks on — if it blocks — will *say
why*, which is the whole point. Wound #22 is the recovery-class sibling of the
[[polite-fakes-franchise-wound]]: the carrier could reach `captured` once, but
could not **recover** a torn drive to `captured`. Following
[[carrier-wound-btoa-latin1-ceiling-2026-06-14]], both surfaced only because the
run was watched node-by-node, not stared at.
